1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:16,103
Look at this java for a moment. That is
the entire Spring footprint of a CLI
freight-rate calculator ops runs from a
laptop on the warehouse floor. No embedded
Tomcat. No message source. No
@EventListener.

2
00:00:16,224 --> 00:00:20,879
Just wiring: lane tariffs, fuel
surcharges, a calculator.

3
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:29,880
The team embedded Spring only because
hand-rolled factories were becoming a
second product — every new surcharge rule
meant another if in a giant FreightFactory
class,

4
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:34,200
and every unit test had to mock
construction order by hand.

5
00:00:34,320 --> 00:00:42,311
BeanFactory is that minimal contract. It
is the root interface for accessing the
Spring container's bean instances.

6
00:00:42,432 --> 00:00:50,184
You register bean definitions — class
name, id, property values, constructor
args — and ask for objects by name or
type.

7
00:00:50,304 --> 00:00:59,760
DefaultListableBeanFactory is the
workhorse implementation: it holds the
definition registry, resolves
dependencies, applies scopes, and caches
singletons.

8
00:00:59,880 --> 00:01:12,192
Almost everything richer in Spring
eventually asks this factory for objects;
ApplicationContext does not replace it —
it wraps more services around the same
core. Walk the freight CLI line by line.

9
00:01:12,311 --> 00:01:24,719
new DefaultListableBeanFactory() builds an
empty registry and singleton cache — no
beans yet, no classpath scanning.
XmlBeanDefinitionReader is a metadata
loader, not the calculator.

10
00:01:24,840 --> 00:01:36,792
loadBeanDefinitions(new
ClassPathResource("freight-rate-
beans.xml")) opens the XML from the
classpath, parses each <bean> element into
a BeanDefinition object, and registers it
under its id.

11
00:01:36,912 --> 00:01:44,544
At this moment the factory knows *what*
could exist; it has not necessarily
constructed anything expensive if beans
stay lazy.

12
00:01:44,664 --> 00:01:55,128
getBean("rateCalculator",
RateCalculator.class) is the first demand:
look up the definition named
rateCalculator, check the singleton cache,
miss, then create.

13
00:01:55,248 --> 00:02:07,103
Creation walks constructor arguments: the
factory sees refs to tariffTable and
fuelSurcharge, creates those first
(loading tariffs.csv into memory for the
table), injects them into RateCalculator,

14
00:02:07,224 --> 00:02:11,376
stores the result in the singleton cache,
and returns it.

15
00:02:11,496 --> 00:02:21,432
calculator.quote(...) is ordinary Java —
Spring is already out of the path. A
second getBean("rateCalculator") returns
the cached instance.

16
00:02:21,552 --> 00:02:31,296
When main exits, the JVM dies; there is no
graceful context-close ceremony unless you
add destroy callbacks yourself. Look at
this example.

17
00:02:31,416 --> 00:02:45,408
<bean id="tariffTable"
class="com.freight.TariffTable"> Each line
of that XML is a BeanDefinition field in
disguise. id is the lookup key. class is
the implementation to instantiate.

18
00:02:45,528 --> 00:02:57,504
The classpath:tariffs.csv constructor arg
becomes a string (or Resource, depending
on conversion) passed into TariffTable's
constructor — the factory does not open
the CSV until that bean is created.

19
00:02:57,624 --> 00:03:06,432
ref="tariffTable" is a dependency edge,
not a nested object literal; the factory
resolves edges by creating or reusing
collaborators.

20
00:03:06,552 --> 00:03:18,024
Order of <bean> elements in the file does
not have to match creation order — the
dependency graph does. Failure mode that
shows up on the warehouse floor: a typo in
a ref, or a missing bean id.

21
00:03:18,144 --> 00:03:29,256
Symptom at getBean("rateCalculator") is
NoSuchBeanDefinitionException or
UnsatisfiedDependencyException naming the
broken ref — often wrapped so the stack
starts in AbstractBeanFactory.

22
00:03:29,376 --> 00:03:43,271
The CLI prints nothing useful to the quote
path; it dies during wiring. Another
symptom: ClassPathResource cannot find
freight-rate-beans.xml →
BeanDefinitionStoreException on
loadBeanDefinitions, before any quote
runs.

23
00:03:43,391 --> 00:03:50,807
Those failures are loud and early, which
is what you want in a batch tool operators
launch by hand. Trade-offs.

24
00:03:50,927 --> 00:04:01,776
A bare BeanFactory keeps the process tiny
and understandable — ideal for CLIs,
custom scopes experiments, and library
code that must embed Spring without
pulling application services.

25
00:04:01,895 --> 00:04:09,935
You pay for that thinness: no built-in
i18n message source, no application-event
multicaster, no Environment post-
processors,

26
00:04:10,055 --> 00:04:16,367
no convenient close() lifecycle for the
whole app unless you wire DisposableBean
callbacks yourself.

27
00:04:16,487 --> 00:04:28,800
You also own bootstrap — readers, resource
locations, when to call getBean.
ApplicationContext buys those services and
a standard refresh lifecycle at the cost
of heavier startup and more moving parts.

28
00:04:28,920 --> 00:04:42,360
Misconception unique to BeanFactory:
"BeanFactory is obsolete; only
ApplicationContext matters." BeanFactory
is not obsolete — it is the substrate.
ApplicationContext extends it.

29
00:04:42,480 --> 00:04:57,024
Tools, custom scopes, and Boot internals
still speak factory. Choosing
ApplicationContext for an app does not
erase the factory; it wraps richer
services around the same getBean core. The
warehouse CLI is happy.

30
00:04:57,144 --> 00:05:08,112
The pharmacy kiosk team next door is not —
they need localized labels on the
touchscreen, environment-specific drug
catalog URLs, and a way to publish
"prescription ready" events to a display
board.

31
00:05:08,232 --> 00:05:18,360
A bare BeanFactory will wire their
services, but it will not give them those
application services for free. Something
richer has to sit on top of the factory.
